{"id": 29, "ts": 1780419524.311364, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "james_carter", "message": "I see that both Sarah and Emily have leaned toward Candidate\u202fC, rightly noting his ability to stay calm in a crisis and to make correct decisions quickly\u2014critical for long\u2011distance operations. However, the same notes also flag that Candidate\u202fC \u201chas difficulty communicating ideas,\u201d is \u201cregarded as egocentric,\u201d and \u201cis not very willing to further his education.\u201d Those traits raise serious concerns about crew cooperation and adaptability, especially on international flights where clear, collaborative communication is non\u2011negotiable.\nCandidate\u202fB, on the other hand, \u201chandles stress very well,\u201d is \u201cvery conscientious,\u201d and \u201cis good at assessing weather conditions,\u201d all strong indicators of operational reliability and technical competence. While he does \u201cmake nasty remarks about his colleagues\u201d and has a \u201crelatively weak memory for numbers,\u201d the former can be addressed through team\u2011building and professional\u2011communication training, and the latter can be mitigated with procedural cross\u2011checks.\nGiven that effective crew interaction outweighs isolated interpersonal lapses, I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fB offers the more balanced fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role."}
